use /lib
use /ui
use /ui/components/sidebar/Panel
from .. import RightSidebarScope
from /scope/open-file import OpenFileScope
from /scope/project-locations import ProjectLocationsScope

export panel ReferencedFilesPanel:
    title: Referenced Spec
    counted: false
    height: fit
    scrolls: true
    scrollbar: overflowing

    concept: {ReferencedFilesConcept}

concept ReferencedFilesConcept:
    pitch:
        The top panel of the @{RightSidebarScope}: the spec files the
        running task references.

    shape: {ReferencedFilesShape}

shape ReferencedFilesShape:
    description:
        One row per spec file the running task references, each listed once: first those its prompt imports from, then
        the rest in the order they are first referenced. The rows sit 8
        pixels in from the panel's edges, above and below as well as at the
        sides, each 24 pixels tall, with no icon: each shows its file name
        alone, in small text, without its folders, cut off with
        an ellipsis when the sidebar is too narrow, its full path in its
        tooltip. A file named index, whatever its extension, such as
        `index.pi`, shows the folder it is in before its name, as
        `chat-input/index.pi`, and no folder above that.

    referencing:
        A spec file is one in the spec location, as the
        @{ProjectLocationsScope} says; a compiled spec file is one under
        `.claude/reference`, which stands for part of the spec. Both are
        listed, and files anywhere else are not. A file's full path counts
        however long it is, and a relative path is read from the project
        directory.

        A file is referenced, however the harness reads it, when:

        - the task's prompt imports from it, its hidden anchor naming it
        - the harness reads, edits, or writes it with a file tool
        - a shell command reads it, such as cat, head, tail, sed, less, grep,
            rg, awk, or diff naming it, or a pattern or folder that takes it in,
            as far as the command shows
        - a shell command edits or writes it, such as sed -i, a redirect (> or
            >>), tee, mv, or cp onto it
        - a search tool, Grep or Glob, searches a folder or pattern that takes
            it in and its results name it
        - a subagent the harness starts does any of the above

        A file taken in only by a folder or pattern, without the results
        naming it, is not listed. A command whose target can't be worked out
        is skipped rather than guessed at. A file referenced again isn't listed
        again.

        A file edited or written in any of these ways, rather than only read
        or imported, shows a small pencil icon at the right of its row. A row
        a subagent referenced shows the same way, with "Read by a subagent" in
        its tooltip beneath its full path.

        Clicking a row opens its file, as the @{OpenFileScope} says. While no
        spec file is referenced yet, the list reads
        "No spec files referenced yet", muted.
